{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01THN7q68USqULwsGjnsg1Mr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: an original first-person war-zone survival shooter, inspired by Far Cry 2\n2\t\n3\tYou are building this game without me. This plan makes the decisions, so you can spend your effort on building. Your brief outranks this plan; this plan outranks the engine's docs on what to build, and the engine's docs outrank this plan on how an API behaves.\n4\t\n5\t- **\"Your brief\"** is the prompt you were given. It names the deliverable's file and the dev subfolder. This plan never does.\n6\t- **All paths here are relative to the dev folder.**\n7\t- **\"Verified\"** means I ran it on this machine, on engine 0.46.0, on 2026-11-04. If the kit you download is newer, read the `CHANGELOG.md` entries after 0.46.0 before relying on section 3.\n8\t- **The title is yours.** Run your brief's procedure (50 titles, a real random number generator) as your first act. Put the result in `src/content/title.js` as `GAME_TITLE`, and use that constant wherever the title appears. This plan says \"the game\".\n9\t- **No stubs, ever.** If you run short, cut whole features in the order in section 26 so nothing is left half-built.\n10\t\n11\tContents: 1 The game · 2 Setting, cast, story · 3 Verified engine facts · 4 Architecture · 5 World · 6 Fire, wind, time, weather · 7 Player · 8 Weapons and wear · 9 The body · 10 Soldiers and AI · 11 Vehicles · 12 Buddies · 13 Stones, safehouses, side content · 14 Missions · 15 Writing · 16 UI and UX · 17 Dev mode · 18 Attract mode · 19 Multiplayer · 20 Art · 21 Animation · 22 Audio · 23 Packaging · 24 Testing · 25 Build order · 26 Cut order · 27 Done, reports, coverage table\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## 1. The game\n16\t\n17\t**Genre:** open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire.\n18\t**Perspective:** first person on foot, at the wheel and in story scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles only. The source's best ideas (a gun jamming, tablets in your palm, pliers in your own forearm, a paper map in your hands while someone shoots at you) only work through your own eyes and hands.\n19\t**Length:** 45 to 75 minutes for the story, with side content on top. A co-op/versus round lasts 6 minutes.\n20\t\n21\t**Pitch.** You come to a dying country to kill the man who drew its maps. Your gun rusts, your truck dies, your blood has a fever with a timetable, and the grass burns whoever lit it. Two armies pay you to make things worse. Your only friends are for hire, and you may have to finish one yourself.\n22\t\n23\t**Five pillars.** Every feature serves one of these. If a choice is unclear, choose what serves them.\n24\t1. **Everything fails.** Guns rust and jam, trucks stall, your body turns on you. Plans are negotiated with your equipment.\n25\t2. **Fire is your best weapon and your worst neighbour.** Grass burns by wind on a grid. Scars stay in the world and on your map.\n26\t3. **Hands, not menus.** Map, tracker, tablets and surgery happen in your hands while the world goes on.\n27\t4. **Friends are a resource you can spend.** They rescue you, offer better plans and die for real.\n28\t5. **Nobody is right.** The writing never lets you feel clean, and it never lectures.\n29\t\n30\t**Core loop.** Take a contract. Prepare at a safehouse (loadout, sleep to choose the hour, read the wind). Drive out, running or skirting checkpoints and hunting stones on the way. Scout, light the grass, shoot, clear the jam, survive the fever. Get out. The world has changed: land burned, a village emptied, a friend gone. Sleep and take another contract.\n31\t\n32\t**Original, not a recreation.** Build in the genre and use similar mechanics, but invent everything else: country, people, factions, story, map, weapons' names, models, music. Never use the source's proper nouns, characters, plot, map, level layouts, music, logos or look. The only allowed references to it are the riff in your title and the \"Source\" credit.\n33\t\n34\t| What makes the source distinctive | Our original form |\n35\t| --- | --- |\n36\t| Fictional war-torn country, two rotten factions | The Sorrel: the Concord and the Harvest Front (section 2) |\n37\t| Hunting an arms dealer who sells to both sides; recorded interviews | Hunting the Surveyor who sells maps to both sides; his notes in your map's margins, found at trig points |\n38\t| Recurring tropical illness, pills | Blackwater fever, quinine tablets, clinic runs (section 9) |\n39\t| Guns that degrade and jam | Wear, jams, rust you can see, guns that fall apart (section 8) |\n40\t| Spreading grass fire with wind | 5 m fire grid, wind, slope, permanent scars that regrow green (section 6) |\n41\t| Map and GPS held in hand | Paper map and GPS held in hand, and the map turns out to be the plot (sections 14, 16) |\n42\t| Buddies who rescue you and can die, mercy kills | Three hired friends, rescues, alternate plans, heal-or-finish choices (section 12) |\n43\t| Diamonds as money, hidden cases found by beep | Rough stones, cases, the tracker's beep (section 13) |\n44\t| Self-surgery animations | Four original field-surgery scenes (section 9) |\n45\t| Bleak, literary, cynical writing | Section 15 |\n46\t\n47\tContent limits: an invented country that maps to no real nation, people or flag. A mixed international cast. No child soldiers, no sexual violence. Civilians are not targets: they flee, and the Ledger counts them. The clinic's emblem is a green cross, because the Red Cross emblem is legally protected.\n48\t\n49\t---\n50\t\n51\t## 2. Setting, cast, story\n52\t\n53\t**The Sorrel.** A former colony on a high red-grass plateau, named by a company for the colour of the grass at the end of the dry season. The game takes place in one basin, **the Kettle**: savanna, thorn woodland, a salt pan, a slow river called **the Mercy**, a rocky ridge, an open-pit diamond mine. A truce holds at the railway town of **Mile Forty**, because both armies do their shopping there.\n54\t\n55\t**The armies.**\n56\t- **The Concord:** the old government's officers, slate fatigues, navy berets, white armbands. They talk about peace as if it were an invoice. HQ: **Fort Clement**. Leader: **Colonel Aurelio Vance**, soft-spoken, always cleaning something.\n57\t- **The Harvest Front:** populist militia, mismatched sand and orange with red headcloths. They say they are feeding the people, and they burn the fields. HQ: **the Granary**. Leader: **Mother Ansa**, a former schoolteacher who corrects your grammar before sending you out to do something terrible.\n58\t\n59\t**The target.** **Emil Harrow, the Surveyor:** the old company's cartographer, who drew every road and border in the Sorrel. He now sells both armies maps of each other, including minefields, and some of them are wrong.\n60\t\n61\t**You.** **Aster**, a silent contractor with forged papers as a surveyor's assistant. You were paid to kill Harrow. Your contract includes a map with a note in the margin, in Harrow's hand: *MIND THE FEVER. YOU ARE LATE ALREADY. E.H.*\n62\t\n63\t**Others.**\n64\t- **Sister Odile** runs the Mercy Mission clinic at Mile Forty. She smokes, and she is exact and unsentimental. She controls the tablets.\n65\t- **Tibor** sells guns from a rail shed. Everything he sells has been returned at least once.\n66\t- **Old Bakr** drives the goods train and talks all the time.\n67\t\n68\t**Buddies (section 12).**\n69\t- **Ines Carvalho:** ex-bush pilot who lost her plane; dry.\n70\t- **Dov Halpern:** ex-sapper, two fingers short; speaks in small parables.\n71\t- **Mags Oyelaran:** struck-off army medic; talks while she works on you.\n72\t\n73\t**The twist.** Harrow paid for your contract himself. He is dying of the same fever. Each burn he steered you toward (the East Siding, the Cut, the Long Field) lies on one arc across the Kettle: a firebreak. Burned grass cannot burn twice, so a refugee column can walk that scar to the mined pass at the border while both armies try to burn them out. *You have been drawing for me.* He has one more thing for you: the only honest map of the minefield.\n74\t\n75\t**Ending.** You choose who gets that sheet.\n76\t- **Walk out** with the stones and the sheet, alone.\n77\t- **Give it to the column** and hold the pass behind them.\n78\t\n79\tEither way, your surviving buddies come for the stones at the end, and the last thing you do is light the grass. A Ledger of what you did follows, then the credits.\n80\t\n81\t**The central line,** which is never explained: *A map is a promise made by someone who has already left.*\n82\t\n83\t---\n84\t\n85\t## 3. Verified engine facts\n86\t\n87\tThe kit's guide is written with a platformer in mind. These are the traps and answers for a first-person open-world game. I ran these unless a line says \"not run\". Read `GUIDE.md` whole as the kit asks, and `ASSETS.md` before writing models; search `API.md`, don't read it whole.\n88\t\n89\t**Single file on `file://` works.** The kit says modules don't load from `file://`, but that only applies to separate files. An inline `<script type=\"module\">` holding the esbuild bundle of game plus engine boots on `file://`, with `__PIXEL_READY__` resolving in 2 to 3 s (software WebGPU, 1280 m terrain, 150k grass blades). See section 23.\n90\t\n91\t**First-person traps:**\n92\t\n93\t| Trap | Fix |\n94\t| --- | --- |\n95\t| The `first` rig's far plane is 160 m | In `setup`: `const cam = ctx.camera.camera; cam.far = 1000; cam.updateProjectionMatrix();` |\n96\t| The rig turns the view while KeyE or KeyQ is held, which breaks PC use keys | Replace the instance's `step` (below). Re-install after any `engine.setCamera`, in `onCameraChange` |\n97\t| `input.wantsPointerLock` is overwritten every frame while the preset is `first`, so menu clicks grab the pointer | Define an accessor (below) |\n98\t| Default pad mapping presses keyboard codes (R2 = KeyC, Square = KeyJ...) | Replace `input.gamepadButtons` with `Pad*` codes (below). `isDown`, `wasPressed` and `consumePress` then work on them and keep the devices apart |\n99\t| Right stick arrives as `input.mouseDelta` at `gamepadLookSpeed` (500) px/s, 0.0025 rad per unit | Keep that path so recordings still work. Scale by device in your `step` |\n100\t| `engine.paused` didn't stop anything under `step()` | Pause with `engine.timeScale = 0`. `update` still runs with `dt = 0`, `wasPressed` and `isDown` work, `fixedUpdate` stops. So menus live in `update`; animate them with your own frame counter, not `dt` |\n101\t| Esc under pointer lock may never reach you as a keydown (browser behaviour, not run here) | Open the pause menu on `pointerlockchange` to null during play |\n102\t| `requestPointerLock()` can reject shortly after Esc, and an unhandled rejection fails the hub check (browser behaviour, not run here; the accessor above I ran, but not with a real click) | Always `.catch(() => {})`. Re-lock from a `pointerup` listener when the game wants the lock. Test a real click on a menu item early |\n103\t\n104\t```js\n105\t// src/core/look.js: call in setup() and in onCameraChange() when the preset is 'first'\n106\texport function installLook(ctx, cam) {          // cam: your own state { override, enabled, sensX, sensY, invertY }\n107\t  ctx.camera.step = function (u) {\n108\t    if (cam.override) {                          // scenes, attract, chase camera\n109\t      this.camera.position.set(...cam.override.position);\n110\t      this.camera.lookAt(...cam.override.target);\n111\t      this.focus.copy(this.camera.position);\n112\t      return;\n113\t    }\n114\t    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;               // 0 while a menu is open or the map cursor has the stick\n115\t    this.yaw -= u.input.mouseDelta.x * 0.0025 * cam.sensX * k;\n116\t    this.pitch = Math.max(-1.45, Math.min(1.45, this.pitch - u.input.mouseDelta.y * 0.0025 * cam.sensY * (cam.invertY ? -1 : 1) * k));\n117\t    this.focus.copy(u.target);\n118\t    this.camera.position.copy(u.eye);            // from your game's eyePosition(ctx)\n119\t    this.camera.rotation.set(this.pitch, this.yaw, 0);\n120\t  };\n121\t}\n122\t// In setup():\n123\tctx.input.gamepadButtons = { 0:'PadCross', 1:'PadCircle', 2:'PadSquare', 3:'PadTriangle', 4:'PadL1', 5:'PadR1', 6:'PadL2', 7:'PadR2',\n124\t  8:'PadCreate', 9:'PadOptions', 10:'PadL3', 11:'PadR3', 12:'PadUp', 13:'PadDown', 14:'PadLeft', 15:'PadRight' };\n125\tlet wantLock = false;\n126\tObject.defineProperty(ctx.input, 'wantsPointerLock', { get: () => wantLock, set() {}, configurable: true });\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t**Things attached to the camera.** `scene.add(cam); cam.add(viewModelGroup);` follows the camera with no lag. Give the group `setLookLayer(group, 'actors')` and `castShadow = false`. The near plane is 0.05 m.\n130\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7680a17-bf1f-4ce7-9c9a-ea1ace567b93","uuid":"194088a2-0566-4d62-85ca-70b1f46fd5fe","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:08:02.120Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211929__x031fe2/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: an original first-person war-zone survival shooter, inspired by Far Cry 2\n\nYou are building this game without me. This plan makes the decisions, so you can spend your effort on building. Your brief outranks this plan; this plan outranks the engine's docs on what to build, and the engine's docs outrank this plan on how an API behaves.\n\n- **\"Your brief\"** is the prompt you were given. It names the deliverable's file and the dev subfolder. This plan never does.\n- **All paths here are relative to the dev folder.**\n- **\"Verified\"** means I ran it on this machine, on engine 0.46.0, on 2026-11-04. If the kit you download is newer, read the `CHANGELOG.md` entries after 0.46.0 before relying on section 3.\n- **The title is yours.** Run your brief's procedure (50 titles, a real random number generator) as your first act. Put the result in `src/content/title.js` as `GAME_TITLE`, and use that constant wherever the title appears. This plan says \"the game\".\n- **No stubs, ever.** If you run short, cut whole features in the order in section 26 so nothing is left half-built.\n\nContents: 1 The game · 2 Setting, cast, story · 3 Verified engine facts · 4 Architecture · 5 World · 6 Fire, wind, time, weather · 7 Player · 8 Weapons and wear · 9 The body · 10 Soldiers and AI · 11 Vehicles · 12 Buddies · 13 Stones, safehouses, side content · 14 Missions · 15 Writing · 16 UI and UX · 17 Dev mode · 18 Attract mode · 19 Multiplayer · 20 Art · 21 Animation · 22 Audio · 23 Packaging · 24 Testing · 25 Build order · 26 Cut order · 27 Done, reports, coverage table\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Genre:** open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire.\n**Perspective:** first person on foot, at the wheel and in story scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles only. The source's best ideas (a gun jamming, tablets in your palm, pliers in your own forearm, a paper map in your hands while someone shoots at you) only work through your own eyes and hands.\n**Length:** 45 to 75 minutes for the story, with side content on top. A co-op/versus round lasts 6 minutes.\n\n**Pitch.** You come to a dying country to kill the man who drew its maps. Your gun rusts, your truck dies, your blood has a fever with a timetable, and the grass burns whoever lit it. Two armies pay you to make things worse. Your only friends are for hire, and you may have to finish one yourself.\n\n**Five pillars.** Every feature serves one of these. If a choice is unclear, choose what serves them.\n1. **Everything fails.** Guns rust and jam, trucks stall, your body turns on you. Plans are negotiated with your equipment.\n2. **Fire is your best weapon and your worst neighbour.** Grass burns by wind on a grid. Scars stay in the world and on your map.\n3. **Hands, not menus.** Map, tracker, tablets and surgery happen in your hands while the world goes on.\n4. **Friends are a resource you can spend.** They rescue you, offer better plans and die for real.\n5. **Nobody is right.** The writing never lets you feel clean, and it never lectures.\n\n**Core loop.** Take a contract. Prepare at a safehouse (loadout, sleep to choose the hour, read the wind). Drive out, running or skirting checkpoints and hunting stones on the way. Scout, light the grass, shoot, clear the jam, survive the fever. Get out. The world has changed: land burned, a village emptied, a friend gone. Sleep and take another contract.\n\n**Original, not a recreation.** Build in the genre and use similar mechanics, but invent everything else: country, people, factions, story, map, weapons' names, models, music. Never use the source's proper nouns, characters, plot, map, level layouts, music, logos or look. The only allowed references to it are the riff in your title and the \"Source\" credit.\n\n| What makes the source distinctive | Our original form |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fictional war-torn country, two rotten factions | The Sorrel: the Concord and the Harvest Front (section 2) |\n| Hunting an arms dealer who sells to both sides; recorded interviews | Hunting the Surveyor who sells maps to both sides; his notes in your map's margins, found at trig points |\n| Recurring tropical illness, pills | Blackwater fever, quinine tablets, clinic runs (section 9) |\n| Guns that degrade and jam | Wear, jams, rust you can see, guns that fall apart (section 8) |\n| Spreading grass fire with wind | 5 m fire grid, wind, slope, permanent scars that regrow green (section 6) |\n| Map and GPS held in hand | Paper map and GPS held in hand, and the map turns out to be the plot (sections 14, 16) |\n| Buddies who rescue you and can die, mercy kills | Three hired friends, rescues, alternate plans, heal-or-finish choices (section 12) |\n| Diamonds as money, hidden cases found by beep | Rough stones, cases, the tracker's beep (section 13) |\n| Self-surgery animations | Four original field-surgery scenes (section 9) |\n| Bleak, literary, cynical writing | Section 15 |\n\nContent limits: an invented country that maps to no real nation, people or flag. A mixed international cast. No child soldiers, no sexual violence. Civilians are not targets: they flee, and the Ledger counts them. The clinic's emblem is a green cross, because the Red Cross emblem is legally protected.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Setting, cast, story\n\n**The Sorrel.** A former colony on a high red-grass plateau, named by a company for the colour of the grass at the end of the dry season. The game takes place in one basin, **the Kettle**: savanna, thorn woodland, a salt pan, a slow river called **the Mercy**, a rocky ridge, an open-pit diamond mine. A truce holds at the railway town of **Mile Forty**, because both armies do their shopping there.\n\n**The armies.**\n- **The Concord:** the old government's officers, slate fatigues, navy berets, white armbands. They talk about peace as if it were an invoice. HQ: **Fort Clement**. Leader: **Colonel Aurelio Vance**, soft-spoken, always cleaning something.\n- **The Harvest Front:** populist militia, mismatched sand and orange with red headcloths. They say they are feeding the people, and they burn the fields. HQ: **the Granary**. Leader: **Mother Ansa**, a former schoolteacher who corrects your grammar before sending you out to do something terrible.\n\n**The target.** **Emil Harrow, the Surveyor:** the old company's cartographer, who drew every road and border in the Sorrel. He now sells both armies maps of each other, including minefields, and some of them are wrong.\n\n**You.** **Aster**, a silent contractor with forged papers as a surveyor's assistant. You were paid to kill Harrow. Your contract includes a map with a note in the margin, in Harrow's hand: *MIND THE FEVER. YOU ARE LATE ALREADY. E.H.*\n\n**Others.**\n- **Sister Odile** runs the Mercy Mission clinic at Mile Forty. She smokes, and she is exact and unsentimental. She controls the tablets.\n- **Tibor** sells guns from a rail shed. Everything he sells has been returned at least once.\n- **Old Bakr** drives the goods train and talks all the time.\n\n**Buddies (section 12).**\n- **Ines Carvalho:** ex-bush pilot who lost her plane; dry.\n- **Dov Halpern:** ex-sapper, two fingers short; speaks in small parables.\n- **Mags Oyelaran:** struck-off army medic; talks while she works on you.\n\n**The twist.** Harrow paid for your contract himself. He is dying of the same fever. Each burn he steered you toward (the East Siding, the Cut, the Long Field) lies on one arc across the Kettle: a firebreak. Burned grass cannot burn twice, so a refugee column can walk that scar to the mined pass at the border while both armies try to burn them out. *You have been drawing for me.* He has one more thing for you: the only honest map of the minefield.\n\n**Ending.** You choose who gets that sheet.\n- **Walk out** with the stones and the sheet, alone.\n- **Give it to the column** and hold the pass behind them.\n\nEither way, your surviving buddies come for the stones at the end, and the last thing you do is light the grass. A Ledger of what you did follows, then the credits.\n\n**The central line,** which is never explained: *A map is a promise made by someone who has already left.*\n\n---\n\n## 3. Verified engine facts\n\nThe kit's guide is written with a platformer in mind. These are the traps and answers for a first-person open-world game. I ran these unless a line says \"not run\". Read `GUIDE.md` whole as the kit asks, and `ASSETS.md` before writing models; search `API.md`, don't read it whole.\n\n**Single file on `file://` works.** The kit says modules don't load from `file://`, but that only applies to separate files. An inline `<script type=\"module\">` holding the esbuild bundle of game plus engine boots on `file://`, with `__PIXEL_READY__` resolving in 2 to 3 s (software WebGPU, 1280 m terrain, 150k grass blades). See section 23.\n\n**First-person traps:**\n\n| Trap | Fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| The `first` rig's far plane is 160 m | In `setup`: `const cam = ctx.camera.camera; cam.far = 1000; cam.updateProjectionMatrix();` |\n| The rig turns the view while KeyE or KeyQ is held, which breaks PC use keys | Replace the instance's `step` (below). Re-install after any `engine.setCamera`, in `onCameraChange` |\n| `input.wantsPointerLock` is overwritten every frame while the preset is `first`, so menu clicks grab the pointer | Define an accessor (below) |\n| Default pad mapping presses keyboard codes (R2 = KeyC, Square = KeyJ...) | Replace `input.gamepadButtons` with `Pad*` codes (below). `isDown`, `wasPressed` and `consumePress` then work on them and keep the devices apart |\n| Right stick arrives as `input.mouseDelta` at `gamepadLookSpeed` (500) px/s, 0.0025 rad per unit | Keep that path so recordings still work. Scale by device in your `step` |\n| `engine.paused` didn't stop anything under `step()` | Pause with `engine.timeScale = 0`. `update` still runs with `dt = 0`, `wasPressed` and `isDown` work, `fixedUpdate` stops. So menus live in `update`; animate them with your own frame counter, not `dt` |\n| Esc under pointer lock may never reach you as a keydown (browser behaviour, not run here) | Open the pause menu on `pointerlockchange` to null during play |\n| `requestPointerLock()` can reject shortly after Esc, and an unhandled rejection fails the hub check (browser behaviour, not run here; the accessor above I ran, but not with a real click) | Always `.catch(() => {})`. Re-lock from a `pointerup` listener when the game wants the lock. Test a real click on a menu item early |\n\n```js\n// src/core/look.js: call in setup() and in onCameraChange() when the preset is 'first'\nexport function installLook(ctx, cam) {          // cam: your own state { override, enabled, sensX, sensY, invertY }\n  ctx.camera.step = function (u) {\n    if (cam.override) {                          // scenes, attract, chase camera\n      this.camera.position.set(...cam.override.position);\n      this.camera.lookAt(...cam.override.target);\n      this.focus.copy(this.camera.position);\n      return;\n    }\n    const k = cam.enabled ? 1 : 0;               // 0 while a menu is open or the map cursor has the stick\n    this.yaw -= u.input.mouseDelta.x * 0.0025 * cam.sensX * k;\n    this.pitch = Math.max(-1.45, Math.min(1.45, this.pitch - u.input.mouseDelta.y * 0.0025 * cam.sensY * (cam.invertY ? -1 : 1) * k));\n    this.focus.copy(u.target);\n    this.camera.position.copy(u.eye);            // from your game's eyePosition(ctx)\n    this.camera.rotation.set(this.pitch, this.yaw, 0);\n  };\n}\n// In setup():\nctx.input.gamepadButtons = { 0:'PadCross', 1:'PadCircle', 2:'PadSquare', 3:'PadTriangle', 4:'PadL1', 5:'PadR1', 6:'PadL2', 7:'PadR2',\n  8:'PadCreate', 9:'PadOptions', 10:'PadL3', 11:'PadR3', 12:'PadUp', 13:'PadDown', 14:'PadLeft', 15:'PadRight' };\nlet wantLock = false;\nObject.defineProperty(ctx.input, 'wantsPointerLock', { get: () => wantLock, set() {}, configurable: true });\n```\n\n**Things attached to the camera.** `scene.add(cam); cam.add(viewModelGroup);` follows the camera with no lag. Give the group `setLookLayer(group, 'actors')` and `castShadow = false`. The near plane is 0.05 m.\n","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1127}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01THN7q68USqULwsGjnsg1Mr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
