{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J6xYzFAqSufCudAtn4rJME","type":"tool_result","content":"798\t9. Tracker beeping to a case in a burnt wreck.\n799\t10. Train with the flatcar MG.\n800\t11. Tibor's rack, a line of his.\n801\t12. The map on a table with the scars glowing.\n802\t13. The title.\n803\t\n804\tHUD shows a `DEMO` tag and *Press [glyph]*. **Any input exits** (key, mouse button or real movement, pad button or stick, tap) back to the screen it came from, after `world.reset()`. The loop must run indefinitely without error. It runs behind the touch gate too. Never in a hub session.\n805\t\n806\t---\n807\t\n808\t## 19. Multiplayer\n809\t\n810\tFollow the AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (read `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`; also the shared-world example `examples/arena.html` beside it). The hub runs invites, lobby, names, clock and results; the game runs the round. The same code also plays when the game is on its own.\n811\t\n812\t**Mode: `shared-world`, 1 to 4 players.** Four mercs in one burning valley, friends until the stones are counted.\n813\t\n814\t**The round, \"Cash Run\" (6 minutes):**\n815\t- The valley around the Cut: the campaign's terrain, with only the Cut, P5 and the Ranger Post live, 16 to 24 soldiers, wind and fire on.\n816\t- Six stone cases in guarded spots, shown by the tracker's beep and a marker when close. One extraction truck on the road south of the pit.\n817\t- Everyone starts at a different road end with `tractor` (cond 70), `flare`, `cane`, 2 syrettes, 2 bottles.\n818\t- Carry one case at a time (speed × 0.85, you can't sprint); walk it to the truck to bank its stones. Most stones wins.\n819\t- Down at 0 HP: you drop the case and bleed for 25 s. Another player can hold Use for 3 s to bring you up (+1 Revive) or just take the case. Bleed out and you return at your road end after 15 s. Friendly fire is on: that's the theme.\n820\t- On foot only, in rounds: no player vehicles.\n821\t- Ends when the time is up, or when all cases are banked or burned. Standings by stones, ties together.\n822\t\n823\t**Session head tags** go in the template (section 23): `hub:session` 1, `hub:session-players` `1-4`, `hub:session-mode` `shared-world`, `hub:session-minutes` `6`, `hub:webgpu` `required`, `hub:session-goal` *Carry the most diamond cases out of the burning mine*, `hub:session-howto` *Stick/WASD move, R2/mouse fire, Square/E use, carry cases to the truck*.\n824\t\n825\t**Boot order** (`src/main.js`). This is the part that makes the hub work:\n826\t```js\n827\tconst info = (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) ? await AIHub.connect({ mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,4], card:true }) : null;\n828\tif (info) {\n829\t  const game = createGame({ session: hubSession(info) });        // mode 'round': no title, no menus, no attract\n830\t  if (info.server) { const e = await Engine.headless(game, {}); AIHub.ready(); setInterval(() => e.step(6, 1/60), 100); }\n831\t  else if (!navigator.gpu) AIHub.fail('This game needs WebGPU');\n832\t  else await Engine.start(game, { ...OPTIONS, seed: info.seed });  // game.ready(ctx) calls AIHub.ready() once, with no key press\n833\t} else { /* normal start: touch check, title */ }\n834\t```\n835\tHandle a rejected `__PIXEL_READY__` by `AIHub.fail(message)`. If `connect` answers `null`, run as on your own.\n836\t\n837\t**`src/net/session.js`:** one interface, three implementations, so round code never knows which: `{ kind, me, players:[{id,name,color}], host, isHost, server, time(), rng(label), send(data,{to,reliable}), on(event,fn), ready(), answer(what), finish(outcome), fail(msg) }`.\n838\t- `hubSession` wraps `AIHub`.\n839\t- `soloSession` is **on its own**: host is you, the other seats are AI mercs (reuse the buddy brain: go to the nearest case, bank it, shoot what shoots you), with your own results screen at the end.\n840\t- `linkSession` is **on its own with a friend on the same computer**: `BroadcastChannel('game-link-v1')` between two windows. Host and Join on the Cash Run menu, a simple lobby, `start` carrying a `Date.now()` time base and a seed. It costs little, reuses everything, and is how two-window play gets tested.\n841\t\n842\tThe main menu's Cash Run offers: Solo with Hired Guns, Host on This Computer, Join on This Computer, plus one line saying friends online play through AI Hub.\n843\t\n844\t**Netcode.** The host runs the world. Each copy moves its own player from its own stick, at once, never waiting for the network, as `arena.html` does.\n845\t\n846\t| Message | Direction | Rate, kind | Content |\n847\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n848\t| `me` | client → host | 12/s, unreliable | seq, position, yaw, pitch, stance, weapon, hp echo |\n849\t| `ev` | client → host | up to 5/s, reliable, batched | shots since last batch (origin, dir, weapon), use, pick up, bank, revive, ignite |\n850\t| `s` | host → all | 10/s, unreliable, under 1,100 bytes | time, players, soldiers (`[id,x,z,yaw,state,hp]`, ints, y from terrain), cases |\n851\t| `w` | host → all | up to 4/s, reliable, batched | fire cells ignited and burnt, damage per player, downs, deaths, explosions, banks, sounds |\n852\t\n853\tThat's at most 17 messages a second per copy; the limit is 20 with bursts of 40, and 16 KB per message. **Drive sends from `setInterval`, not the render loop:** on a software-rendered copy `update` runs about twice a second, and traffic must still flow.\n854\t\n855\tClients render soldiers as puppets interpolated 150 ms behind; they run fire visuals only; health is host-authoritative. Local shots show muzzle flash and tracers at once, and the host decides hits. Older snapshots are dropped by `at`. In a shared world use `AIHub.rng(label)`, because `Math.random` isn't seeded there; the world is the same static terrain on all copies anyway.\n856\t\n857\t**The server case.** `AIHub.server` true: run the same host code under `Engine.headless` with no player, camera, audio or HUD (`me` isn't in `players`). Don't touch `info.players[me]`.\n858\t\n859\t**Round rules the harness checks:**\n860\t- `time-up`: the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` **directly from the event handler**, within `graceMs` (5 s). Every present player appears once.\n861\t- `leave`: continue; the host finishes if nobody is left.\n862\t- `end`: no more input or sends, `audio.stopMusic()`, `audio.stopLoops()`, mute. The hub shows results.\n863\t- `stats` at most 6 names per player (Stones, Downs, Revives, Kills); `awards` up to 3 (*Last Friend* for most revives, *Paid in Full* for most stones, *Arsonist* for most cells lit).\n864\t\n865\t**In a session:** no title, menus or prompts before the round. No dialogs (`alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`), no `window.open`, no navigation, no network except the hub. Quit does nothing. Names come only from the hub (sanitize to the font's characters; HUD canvas, never HTML). No chat, emotes or custom names. **On a pad, ignore Options while Create is held** (Create + Options is the hub's). Sessions get an empty storage, so settings start at defaults, music at 10%.\n866\t\n867\t**The game's own card** (`card:true`). A shared-world card on your live scene with `GAME_TITLE`, the players in their seat colours with ready marks, how to win, this player's controls in their glyphs, time left from the `card` event. Ready: Enter, Cross, or a tap/click on Ready → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. More time: Triangle, key `M`, or tap → `AIHub.answer('more')`. It has no menus, and play begins only at `start`.\n868\t**Listen for Enter and M with a plain `window` keydown listener** (verified to pass the harness's card check), because the harness expects an answer within 3 s and the render loop may be slow. Pad buttons can poll in `update`. Everything on the card uses `AIHub.rng('card')` for randomness. When `AIHub.time() >= 0` the round is on; show the 3-2-1 countdown before that.\n869\t\n870\t**Fallback.** If shared-world can't be made stable, ship `ghost` (each copy its own world, others' mercs shown, `progress` and `result` by score, `Math.random` seeded for you) rather than drop multiplayer, and last of all `parallel`.\n871\t\n872\t**Test it:** section 24, all four harness scenarios, and two windows on `linkSession`, plus the game outside a session.\n873\t\n874\t---\n875\t\n876\t## 20. Art\n877\t\n878\t**Base style:** the engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art pipeline at 480×270, toon materials, palette colours only. Make new assets for what makes the game feel like its source. The library covers generic props (use it: `crate`, `barrel`, `sack`, `table`, `chair`, `bed`, `stool`, `boulders`, `rock`, `dead-tree`, `bush`, `reeds`, `stone-wall`, `battlement`, `signpost`, `notice-board`, `market-stall`, `well`, `lamp-post`, `campfire`, `rubble`, `mine-rail`, `shovel`). The library has **no guns, vehicles, acacias or modern buildings**: you write those.\n879\t\n880\t**Workflow** (`ASSETS.md`): plain data in `src/art/models.js`; `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js`; open the PNGs; fix; then check in game. Don't re-check what hasn't changed.\n881\t\n882\t**You must make, as `model()` defs:**\n883\t- **People:** soldier (Concord, Harvest; 3 hat variants, 4 `skin` values), mercs (Ines, Dov, Mags, a silhouette each), Odile (veil, cigarette), Tibor (apron), Vance (peaked cap, medals), Ansa (headscarf, spectacles), Harrow (blanket, wide hat), Old Bakr, civilians (4 palettes), all on the rig from section 3.\n884\t- **First-person:** arms and hands (sleeve, skin); `cane`, `m38`, `flare`, `tractor`, `mule`, `empire`, `weed` with tank and hose, `tube`, bottle, charge, detonator; each gun with named rust and tape parts; syrette, tablet bottle, pliers, knife, lighter, wrench, monocular, tracker, map plane, GPS; steering wheel and dash for each vehicle.\n885\t- **Vehicles:** pickup, technical, cargo truck, tanker, burnt wreck palettes of each, locomotive, flatcar, boxcar, handcar as dressing.\n886\t- **Buildings and props:** tin-roof shack, concrete bunker, barracks, watchtower, sandbag run, boom gate, fuel tank, oil drum, silo, water tower, pump station, clinic tent with a green cross, station platform and signal mast, trig pillar, radio mast, mine headframe, sluice house, spoil heap, cage, termite mound, Observatory dome.\n887\t- **Nature:** 3 acacia shapes, bottle tree, scorched versions, thorn bush recolour.\n888\t- **Textures** (as `textureDef`): `grass-flame`, smoke puff if particles aren't enough, rusted metal and tin (or the library's `rust`, `metal-plates`), cracked earth and thatch from the library.\n889\t\n890\t**Colour direction:** sand, orange and red land; plum shadows; slate and white for the Concord; orange and red for the Harvest; green only where water or new growth is, so regrowth reads as an event. Night is navy and ink with fire as the only warm colour.\n891\t\n892\t**Style rules that matter here:** thin parts must be at least 0.06 m and silhouette parts 0.12 m at world scale, but first-person models are held 0.5 m from the eye, so they may be thinner. Set `floats:true`, `collider:'none'` on view models and people. Static scenery merges with `mergeStaticMeshes` where you can.\n893\t\n894\t---\n895\t\n896\t## 21. Animation\n897\t\n898\t**Base:** the engine's native system. `ClipDef` data at 30 fps, `compileClips`, `THREE.AnimationMixer`, `gaitClip` for walks and runs, `Ragdoll`, `SpringChain`, `Squash`, `twoBoneIK`, tweens for small things, `Timeline` for scenes.\n899\t\n900\t**From `HERO_CLIPS`, reused on all people:** Idle, IdleLook, Walk, Run, Crouch, CrouchWalk, Crawl, Sit, SitDown, StandUp, LieIdle, Sleep, Hurt, HitReact, Death, Wave, Shout, Push, Climb. `analyzeClip` and `validateClip` check your own.\n901\t\n902\t**New, on the people rig** (`src/art/clips.js`):\n903\t- **Upper-body, armed versions:** AimIdle, AimWalk (arms holding the rifle, played over the legs), FireRecoil, Reload, ThrowBottle.\n904\t- **New whole-body clips:** BurnPanic (arms flailing, a stumbling run), Beg, Surrender, WoundedSit (clutching a side), DragBackward, CarryOver, DriveSit, UnjamSwear, WalkHandsTied, Shrug (Tibor), SmokeIdle (Odile), CleanRifleIdle (Vance), WriteAtDesk (Harrow), TeachPoint (Ansa).\n905\t\n906\t**To layer upper over lower,** split clips by filtering tracks: `new THREE.AnimationClip(name, clip.duration, clip.tracks.filter(t => /^(Pelvis|Leg|Shin|Foot)/.test(t.name)))`, and the rest for the upper body, then play one of each.\n907\t\n908\t**New, on the hands rig** (`src/art/viewmodels.js` + clips):\n909\t- Idle sway and walk bob (procedural), ADS raise, fire kick per gun.\n910\t- Reload per gun (magazine out and in on `Mag`, bolt on `Bolt`; revolver opens; shells for `mule`; flare breaks open).\n911\t- **Unjam** (slap and rack), **falls apart**, equip and holster.\n912\t- Cane knife swing, throw, plant, detonator click.\n913\t- Tablet to mouth, syrette, **four surgery scenes**, **raise map** and tracker, monocular, wrench on bonnet, hand on wheel and shifter, finish-it.\n914\t\n915\t**Fauna:** gazelle run and graze on its own rig; vulture wing flap.\n916\t\n917\t**Scenes:** use `Timeline` for the M0 ride, the rescue drag, M9, and endings, as `cam.override` plus `lines` and `bars()`. Skip with Cross/Enter (`timeline.skip()`).\n918\t\n919\t---\n920\t\n921\t## 22. Audio\n922\t\n923\tEverything is written in code: `SoundDef` data, `Song` data and generated voices. No audio files. Browsers start sound on the first gesture (the engine handles it; `audio.unlock()` from your own first input). Silent before that, which is fine for attract.\n924\t\n925\t**Music defaults to 10%:** on first boot with no saved settings, `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)`. The Music slider shows 10%. Your Settings Reset returns it to 0.1 (not `resetVolumes()`, which gives 0.6). Test: clean storage → `audio.volume.music === 0.1`.\n926\t\n927\t**Music** (`Song` data, written for this game, original melodies):\n928\t- At least 6: Title, Day (sparse plucked triangle over a low drone), Night (almost nothing), Stalk (tense pulse), Fire and Fight (hand-drum noise, driving low saw), Fever (a detuned Day), End (one line, unaccompanied).\n929\t- Style: plucked pentatonic lines, thumb-piano timbres (triangle or sine, short decay), noise-based hand drums, low drones; nothing that imitates another game's score.\n930\t- Switch on state with a 12 s minimum dwell, using `audio.playMusic`.\n931\t\n932\t**Effects** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, `audio.register`, layered `SoundDef`s). Every one of these is required:\n933\t- Each gun (noise crack + low thump + a tail, plus a distant muffled variant), jam click, slap, rack, magazine, shell, revolver cylinder, flare pop and hiss, flamethrower roar loop, bottle smash, rocket.\n934\t- Explosion, ricochet, bullet whip, hit flesh, hit metal, gun falling apart.\n935\t- Footsteps by surface (dirt, grass, road, wood, water).\n936\t- Breath, heartbeat, syrette hiss, pliers, knife, finger snap, lighter, tablet bottle, grunt.\n937\t- Fire loop, wind loop, night insects, day birds and cicadas (chirps from arps and vibrato).\n938\t- Engine loop (pitch by speed), gravel, brakes, door, horn, starter cough, wrench, train clack, train whistle.\n939\t- Map paper, tracker beep, radio squelch.\n940\t- Thunder, rain loop, distant gunfire stingers.\n941\t- UI move, confirm, back.\n942\t\n943\tSpatialize with `spatialize` and `voice.set({ muffle })` when blocked. A test renders them all with `renderSound` and checks they aren't silent.\n944\t\n945\t**Voices** (`src/audio/voice.js`): Animalese/Simlish gibberish.\n946\t- For each character: base pitch, syllable rate, wave. A syllable is a short layered `SoundDef`, verified to work: `{ wave:'saw', freq:190, freqEnd:170, attack:.005, decay:.09, volume:.3, layers:[{wave:'sine',freq:760,decay:.08,volume:.18},{wave:'sine',freq:1220,decay:.07,volume:.1},{wave:'noise',freq:6000,decay:.012,volume:.08}] }`.\n947\t- Build about 12 of them (vowel formant pairs × consonant noise).\n948\t- `voice.say(character, text)`: for each word, count vowels, hash the word to pick syllables (so a word always \"sounds\" the same), play at the character's pitch with `pitch` offsets for intonation (rise on `?`, fall on `.`, pause at `,`), at the Voices slider's level.\n949\t- Runs over the subtitle's length; soldiers' barks are spatial.\n950\t\n951\tCheck sound by what Web Audio produces (section 24); nobody can listen here.\n952\t\n953\t---\n954\t\n955\t## 23. Packaging\n956\t\n957\tThe deliverable is **one HTML file** at the place your brief names, beside the dev folder. Nothing else is needed at runtime; no CDN is used. It must run from `file://` and from any web server.\n958\t\n959\t**Head order is strict:**\n960\t```html\n961\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n962\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n963\t<script>/* vendor/session.js pasted unmodified, as the first script */</script>\n964\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> ... the tags from section 19 ...\n965\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n966\t<title>GAME_TITLE</title>\n967\t</head><body><div id=\"app\"></div><script type=\"module\">/* the bundle */</script></body></html>\n968\t```\n969\t\n970\t`tools/build.mjs` (the steps I ran, with `</script` escaped; set `OUT` from your brief):\n971\t```js\n972\tconst js = execFileSync('esbuild', ['src/main.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--target=esnext','--legal-comments=inline','--log-level=warning'], { maxBuffer: 1 << 29 }).toString();\n973\tconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js','utf8');\n974\tconst html = TEMPLATE.replace('%SESSION%', () => session.replace(/<\\/script/gi,'<\\\\/script')).replace('%BUNDLE%', () => js.replace(/<\\/script/gi,'<\\\\/script'));\n975\twriteFileSync(OUT, html);\n976\t```\n977\tUse function replacers as above, because the engine contains `$` sequences. The bundle is about 5.3 MB before your game is added. Append the licence texts of the engine, three.js and Rapier from the kit's `CREDITS.md` as an HTML comment at the end of the file. esbuild bundles `pixel-engine.js` without you editing it; that satisfies \"keep the engine as it ships\".\n978\t\n979\t`src/main.js` is the only module that starts the engine. Start options:\n980\t```js\n981\tconst OPTIONS = withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera:{ preset:'first', fov:62 }, look: LOOK, touch:false, settings:{ button:false, key:false } });\n982\t```\n983\t`withUrlOptions` keeps the review flags (`?seed=`, `?debug=1`, `?look=`), which are useful and harmless.\n984\t\n985\t---\n986\t\n987\t## 24. Testing\n988\t\n989\tTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\n990\t\n991\t**Suites:**\n992\t\n993\t1. **`static` (Node):** `Math.random` only in `vendor/`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`; every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\n994\t\n995\t2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\n996\t   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\n997\t   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\n998\t   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\n999\t   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\n1000\t   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\n1001\t   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\n1002\t   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\n1003\t   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\n1004\t   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\n1005\t   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\n1006\t   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\n1007\t   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\n1008\t\n1009\t3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\n1010\t\n1011\t4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\n1012\t\n1013\t5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\n1014\t   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\n1015\t   Check:\n1016\t   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\n1017\t   - `AIHub.active` false\n1018\t   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\n1019\t   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\n1020\t   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\n1021\t   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\n1022\t\n1023\t6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** serve a folder holding the built file (renamed or linked as you like) with `vendor/hub/*` on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=<file>&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\n1024\t   - `players=2&auto=1`\n1025\t   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n1026\t   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\n1027\t   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\n1028\t   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\n1029\t\n1030\t7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\n1031\t\n1032\t**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\n1033\t\n1034\t**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\n1035\t\n1036\t---\n1037\t\n1038\t## 25. Build order\n1039\t\n1040\tEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\n1041\t\n1042\t| Step | Do | Done when |\n1043\t| --- | --- | --- |\n1044\t| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\n1045\t| 1 Walk | Terrain, roads, river, sky, wind, grass, trees. Player, `installLook`, actions, pad codes, device tracking, HUD basics, pause | You can walk the Kettle by pad or mouse |\n1046\t| 2 Shoot | Hands rig and `tractor` first, soldier rig, one AI squad, damage, health, syrette, wear, jam, reload. SFX for each | A fight at P2 is fun on its own. Add the other guns |\n1047\t| 3 Burn | Fire grid, flare, bottle, flamethrower, scars, regrowth, soldier panic, day and night. Fire sounds | Lighting a field at night makes you stop and watch |\n1048\t| 4 Drive | Vehicles, driving view, damage, repair, patrols, posts, train | Run a checkpoint in a pickup and break down after |\n1049\t| 5 World | All sites, safehouses, Tibor, stones, tracker, fever, tablets, surgery, monocular, held map, rail halts, trig points, save and load | A whole session without missions works |\n1050\t| 6 Story | Mission runner, dialogue, voices, buddies, rescues, M0 to End, Ledger, endings. Playthrough suite | A bot and you both finish it |\n1051\t| 7 Shell | Title, all menus, settings, glyph sets, About, touch gate, attract, dev mode | Every screen works by pad, keyboard and mouse |\n1052\t| 8 Multiplayer | Sim/view puppets, host, client, sessions, Cash Run, card, solo bots, link | Hub and link suites pass with a real round |\n1053\t| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\n1054\t| 10 Ship | Final build, About pages, exit interview, chat reply, `node tests/run.mjs --all` | Section 27 is all true |\n1055\t\n1056\t---\n1057\t\n1058\t## 26. Cut order\n1059\t\n1060\tIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\n1061\t\n1062\t1. Fauna (vultures first).\n1063\t2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\n1064\t3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\n1065\t4. Chase camera; dust weather.\n1066\t5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\n1067\t6. M5 (keep its Mags plan in M6 instead).\n1068\t7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\n1069\t8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\n1070\t9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\n1071\t\n1072\tNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\n1073\t\n1074\t---\n1075\t\n1076\t## 27. Done, reports, coverage\n1077\t\n1078\t**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\n1079\t- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\n1080\t- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\n1081\t- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\n1082\t- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\n1083\t- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\n1084\t- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\n1085\t- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\n1086\t- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\n1087\t- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit's.\n1088\t\n1089\t**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\n1090\t- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n1091\t- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\n1092\t- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\".\n1093\t\n1094\t**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\n1095\t\n1096\t**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\n1097\t\n1098\t**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\n1099\t\n1100\t| Brief item | Section |\n1101\t| --- | --- |\n1102\t| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\n1103\t| Genre, perspective | 1 |\n1104\t| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\n1105\t| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\n1106\t| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\n1107\t| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\n1108\t| Art direction: native base, new assets, library for generic props | 20 |\n1109\t| Animation: native base, new animations | 21 |\n1110\t| Audio in code, Animalese voices, music 10% | 22 |\n1111\t| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 19 |\n1112\t| Dev subfolder, what not to read, shared ports and processes | 4, 24 |\n1113\t| Performance out of scope | 3 |\n1114\t| Testing: headless, scripted playthrough, one command, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | 24 |\n1115\t| Effort | Yours to spend; 25 and 26 order it |\n1116\t| This computer and its stand-ins | 3, 4, 24 |\n1117\t| Feature-complete vertical slice, infrastructure for later build-out | 4, 14, 25 |\n1118\t| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | 17 |\n1119\t| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits | 18 |\n1120\t| Narrative tone | 15 |\n1121\t| 10-foot controller first, native keyboard and mouse, hover focus, glyphs, Xbox option | 16 |\n1122\t| No dense web-style screens | 16 |\n1123\t| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | 16, 18 |\n1124\t| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | top, 26, 27 |\n1125\t| Source Information, feedback sections, chat reply, About screen | 27 |\n1126\t| Exit interview | 27 |\n1127\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7680a17-bf1f-4ce7-9c9a-ea1ace567b93","uuid":"bf1370dd-8d8d-42f1-8990-f26b58a01d26","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:08:41.123Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211929__x031fe2/work/PLAN.md","content":"9. Tracker beeping to a case in a burnt wreck.\n10. Train with the flatcar MG.\n11. Tibor's rack, a line of his.\n12. The map on a table with the scars glowing.\n13. The title.\n\nHUD shows a `DEMO` tag and *Press [glyph]*. **Any input exits** (key, mouse button or real movement, pad button or stick, tap) back to the screen it came from, after `world.reset()`. The loop must run indefinitely without error. It runs behind the touch gate too. Never in a hub session.\n\n---\n\n## 19. Multiplayer\n\nFollow the AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (read `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`; also the shared-world example `examples/arena.html` beside it). The hub runs invites, lobby, names, clock and results; the game runs the round. The same code also plays when the game is on its own.\n\n**Mode: `shared-world`, 1 to 4 players.** Four mercs in one burning valley, friends until the stones are counted.\n\n**The round, \"Cash Run\" (6 minutes):**\n- The valley around the Cut: the campaign's terrain, with only the Cut, P5 and the Ranger Post live, 16 to 24 soldiers, wind and fire on.\n- Six stone cases in guarded spots, shown by the tracker's beep and a marker when close. One extraction truck on the road south of the pit.\n- Everyone starts at a different road end with `tractor` (cond 70), `flare`, `cane`, 2 syrettes, 2 bottles.\n- Carry one case at a time (speed × 0.85, you can't sprint); walk it to the truck to bank its stones. Most stones wins.\n- Down at 0 HP: you drop the case and bleed for 25 s. Another player can hold Use for 3 s to bring you up (+1 Revive) or just take the case. Bleed out and you return at your road end after 15 s. Friendly fire is on: that's the theme.\n- On foot only, in rounds: no player vehicles.\n- Ends when the time is up, or when all cases are banked or burned. Standings by stones, ties together.\n\n**Session head tags** go in the template (section 23): `hub:session` 1, `hub:session-players` `1-4`, `hub:session-mode` `shared-world`, `hub:session-minutes` `6`, `hub:webgpu` `required`, `hub:session-goal` *Carry the most diamond cases out of the burning mine*, `hub:session-howto` *Stick/WASD move, R2/mouse fire, Square/E use, carry cases to the truck*.\n\n**Boot order** (`src/main.js`). This is the part that makes the hub work:\n```js\nconst info = (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) ? await AIHub.connect({ mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,4], card:true }) : null;\nif (info) {\n  const game = createGame({ session: hubSession(info) });        // mode 'round': no title, no menus, no attract\n  if (info.server) { const e = await Engine.headless(game, {}); AIHub.ready(); setInterval(() => e.step(6, 1/60), 100); }\n  else if (!navigator.gpu) AIHub.fail('This game needs WebGPU');\n  else await Engine.start(game, { ...OPTIONS, seed: info.seed });  // game.ready(ctx) calls AIHub.ready() once, with no key press\n} else { /* normal start: touch check, title */ }\n```\nHandle a rejected `__PIXEL_READY__` by `AIHub.fail(message)`. If `connect` answers `null`, run as on your own.\n\n**`src/net/session.js`:** one interface, three implementations, so round code never knows which: `{ kind, me, players:[{id,name,color}], host, isHost, server, time(), rng(label), send(data,{to,reliable}), on(event,fn), ready(), answer(what), finish(outcome), fail(msg) }`.\n- `hubSession` wraps `AIHub`.\n- `soloSession` is **on its own**: host is you, the other seats are AI mercs (reuse the buddy brain: go to the nearest case, bank it, shoot what shoots you), with your own results screen at the end.\n- `linkSession` is **on its own with a friend on the same computer**: `BroadcastChannel('game-link-v1')` between two windows. Host and Join on the Cash Run menu, a simple lobby, `start` carrying a `Date.now()` time base and a seed. It costs little, reuses everything, and is how two-window play gets tested.\n\nThe main menu's Cash Run offers: Solo with Hired Guns, Host on This Computer, Join on This Computer, plus one line saying friends online play through AI Hub.\n\n**Netcode.** The host runs the world. Each copy moves its own player from its own stick, at once, never waiting for the network, as `arena.html` does.\n\n| Message | Direction | Rate, kind | Content |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `me` | client → host | 12/s, unreliable | seq, position, yaw, pitch, stance, weapon, hp echo |\n| `ev` | client → host | up to 5/s, reliable, batched | shots since last batch (origin, dir, weapon), use, pick up, bank, revive, ignite |\n| `s` | host → all | 10/s, unreliable, under 1,100 bytes | time, players, soldiers (`[id,x,z,yaw,state,hp]`, ints, y from terrain), cases |\n| `w` | host → all | up to 4/s, reliable, batched | fire cells ignited and burnt, damage per player, downs, deaths, explosions, banks, sounds |\n\nThat's at most 17 messages a second per copy; the limit is 20 with bursts of 40, and 16 KB per message. **Drive sends from `setInterval`, not the render loop:** on a software-rendered copy `update` runs about twice a second, and traffic must still flow.\n\nClients render soldiers as puppets interpolated 150 ms behind; they run fire visuals only; health is host-authoritative. Local shots show muzzle flash and tracers at once, and the host decides hits. Older snapshots are dropped by `at`. In a shared world use `AIHub.rng(label)`, because `Math.random` isn't seeded there; the world is the same static terrain on all copies anyway.\n\n**The server case.** `AIHub.server` true: run the same host code under `Engine.headless` with no player, camera, audio or HUD (`me` isn't in `players`). Don't touch `info.players[me]`.\n\n**Round rules the harness checks:**\n- `time-up`: the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` **directly from the event handler**, within `graceMs` (5 s). Every present player appears once.\n- `leave`: continue; the host finishes if nobody is left.\n- `end`: no more input or sends, `audio.stopMusic()`, `audio.stopLoops()`, mute. The hub shows results.\n- `stats` at most 6 names per player (Stones, Downs, Revives, Kills); `awards` up to 3 (*Last Friend* for most revives, *Paid in Full* for most stones, *Arsonist* for most cells lit).\n\n**In a session:** no title, menus or prompts before the round. No dialogs (`alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`), no `window.open`, no navigation, no network except the hub. Quit does nothing. Names come only from the hub (sanitize to the font's characters; HUD canvas, never HTML). No chat, emotes or custom names. **On a pad, ignore Options while Create is held** (Create + Options is the hub's). Sessions get an empty storage, so settings start at defaults, music at 10%.\n\n**The game's own card** (`card:true`). A shared-world card on your live scene with `GAME_TITLE`, the players in their seat colours with ready marks, how to win, this player's controls in their glyphs, time left from the `card` event. Ready: Enter, Cross, or a tap/click on Ready → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. More time: Triangle, key `M`, or tap → `AIHub.answer('more')`. It has no menus, and play begins only at `start`.\n**Listen for Enter and M with a plain `window` keydown listener** (verified to pass the harness's card check), because the harness expects an answer within 3 s and the render loop may be slow. Pad buttons can poll in `update`. Everything on the card uses `AIHub.rng('card')` for randomness. When `AIHub.time() >= 0` the round is on; show the 3-2-1 countdown before that.\n\n**Fallback.** If shared-world can't be made stable, ship `ghost` (each copy its own world, others' mercs shown, `progress` and `result` by score, `Math.random` seeded for you) rather than drop multiplayer, and last of all `parallel`.\n\n**Test it:** section 24, all four harness scenarios, and two windows on `linkSession`, plus the game outside a session.\n\n---\n\n## 20. Art\n\n**Base style:** the engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art pipeline at 480×270, toon materials, palette colours only. Make new assets for what makes the game feel like its source. The library covers generic props (use it: `crate`, `barrel`, `sack`, `table`, `chair`, `bed`, `stool`, `boulders`, `rock`, `dead-tree`, `bush`, `reeds`, `stone-wall`, `battlement`, `signpost`, `notice-board`, `market-stall`, `well`, `lamp-post`, `campfire`, `rubble`, `mine-rail`, `shovel`). The library has **no guns, vehicles, acacias or modern buildings**: you write those.\n\n**Workflow** (`ASSETS.md`): plain data in `src/art/models.js`; `node check.mjs --sheets src/art/models.js`; open the PNGs; fix; then check in game. Don't re-check what hasn't changed.\n\n**You must make, as `model()` defs:**\n- **People:** soldier (Concord, Harvest; 3 hat variants, 4 `skin` values), mercs (Ines, Dov, Mags, a silhouette each), Odile (veil, cigarette), Tibor (apron), Vance (peaked cap, medals), Ansa (headscarf, spectacles), Harrow (blanket, wide hat), Old Bakr, civilians (4 palettes), all on the rig from section 3.\n- **First-person:** arms and hands (sleeve, skin); `cane`, `m38`, `flare`, `tractor`, `mule`, `empire`, `weed` with tank and hose, `tube`, bottle, charge, detonator; each gun with named rust and tape parts; syrette, tablet bottle, pliers, knife, lighter, wrench, monocular, tracker, map plane, GPS; steering wheel and dash for each vehicle.\n- **Vehicles:** pickup, technical, cargo truck, tanker, burnt wreck palettes of each, locomotive, flatcar, boxcar, handcar as dressing.\n- **Buildings and props:** tin-roof shack, concrete bunker, barracks, watchtower, sandbag run, boom gate, fuel tank, oil drum, silo, water tower, pump station, clinic tent with a green cross, station platform and signal mast, trig pillar, radio mast, mine headframe, sluice house, spoil heap, cage, termite mound, Observatory dome.\n- **Nature:** 3 acacia shapes, bottle tree, scorched versions, thorn bush recolour.\n- **Textures** (as `textureDef`): `grass-flame`, smoke puff if particles aren't enough, rusted metal and tin (or the library's `rust`, `metal-plates`), cracked earth and thatch from the library.\n\n**Colour direction:** sand, orange and red land; plum shadows; slate and white for the Concord; orange and red for the Harvest; green only where water or new growth is, so regrowth reads as an event. Night is navy and ink with fire as the only warm colour.\n\n**Style rules that matter here:** thin parts must be at least 0.06 m and silhouette parts 0.12 m at world scale, but first-person models are held 0.5 m from the eye, so they may be thinner. Set `floats:true`, `collider:'none'` on view models and people. Static scenery merges with `mergeStaticMeshes` where you can.\n\n---\n\n## 21. Animation\n\n**Base:** the engine's native system. `ClipDef` data at 30 fps, `compileClips`, `THREE.AnimationMixer`, `gaitClip` for walks and runs, `Ragdoll`, `SpringChain`, `Squash`, `twoBoneIK`, tweens for small things, `Timeline` for scenes.\n\n**From `HERO_CLIPS`, reused on all people:** Idle, IdleLook, Walk, Run, Crouch, CrouchWalk, Crawl, Sit, SitDown, StandUp, LieIdle, Sleep, Hurt, HitReact, Death, Wave, Shout, Push, Climb. `analyzeClip` and `validateClip` check your own.\n\n**New, on the people rig** (`src/art/clips.js`):\n- **Upper-body, armed versions:** AimIdle, AimWalk (arms holding the rifle, played over the legs), FireRecoil, Reload, ThrowBottle.\n- **New whole-body clips:** BurnPanic (arms flailing, a stumbling run), Beg, Surrender, WoundedSit (clutching a side), DragBackward, CarryOver, DriveSit, UnjamSwear, WalkHandsTied, Shrug (Tibor), SmokeIdle (Odile), CleanRifleIdle (Vance), WriteAtDesk (Harrow), TeachPoint (Ansa).\n\n**To layer upper over lower,** split clips by filtering tracks: `new THREE.AnimationClip(name, clip.duration, clip.tracks.filter(t => /^(Pelvis|Leg|Shin|Foot)/.test(t.name)))`, and the rest for the upper body, then play one of each.\n\n**New, on the hands rig** (`src/art/viewmodels.js` + clips):\n- Idle sway and walk bob (procedural), ADS raise, fire kick per gun.\n- Reload per gun (magazine out and in on `Mag`, bolt on `Bolt`; revolver opens; shells for `mule`; flare breaks open).\n- **Unjam** (slap and rack), **falls apart**, equip and holster.\n- Cane knife swing, throw, plant, detonator click.\n- Tablet to mouth, syrette, **four surgery scenes**, **raise map** and tracker, monocular, wrench on bonnet, hand on wheel and shifter, finish-it.\n\n**Fauna:** gazelle run and graze on its own rig; vulture wing flap.\n\n**Scenes:** use `Timeline` for the M0 ride, the rescue drag, M9, and endings, as `cam.override` plus `lines` and `bars()`. Skip with Cross/Enter (`timeline.skip()`).\n\n---\n\n## 22. Audio\n\nEverything is written in code: `SoundDef` data, `Song` data and generated voices. No audio files. Browsers start sound on the first gesture (the engine handles it; `audio.unlock()` from your own first input). Silent before that, which is fine for attract.\n\n**Music defaults to 10%:** on first boot with no saved settings, `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)`. The Music slider shows 10%. Your Settings Reset returns it to 0.1 (not `resetVolumes()`, which gives 0.6). Test: clean storage → `audio.volume.music === 0.1`.\n\n**Music** (`Song` data, written for this game, original melodies):\n- At least 6: Title, Day (sparse plucked triangle over a low drone), Night (almost nothing), Stalk (tense pulse), Fire and Fight (hand-drum noise, driving low saw), Fever (a detuned Day), End (one line, unaccompanied).\n- Style: plucked pentatonic lines, thumb-piano timbres (triangle or sine, short decay), noise-based hand drums, low drones; nothing that imitates another game's score.\n- Switch on state with a 12 s minimum dwell, using `audio.playMusic`.\n\n**Effects** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, `audio.register`, layered `SoundDef`s). Every one of these is required:\n- Each gun (noise crack + low thump + a tail, plus a distant muffled variant), jam click, slap, rack, magazine, shell, revolver cylinder, flare pop and hiss, flamethrower roar loop, bottle smash, rocket.\n- Explosion, ricochet, bullet whip, hit flesh, hit metal, gun falling apart.\n- Footsteps by surface (dirt, grass, road, wood, water).\n- Breath, heartbeat, syrette hiss, pliers, knife, finger snap, lighter, tablet bottle, grunt.\n- Fire loop, wind loop, night insects, day birds and cicadas (chirps from arps and vibrato).\n- Engine loop (pitch by speed), gravel, brakes, door, horn, starter cough, wrench, train clack, train whistle.\n- Map paper, tracker beep, radio squelch.\n- Thunder, rain loop, distant gunfire stingers.\n- UI move, confirm, back.\n\nSpatialize with `spatialize` and `voice.set({ muffle })` when blocked. A test renders them all with `renderSound` and checks they aren't silent.\n\n**Voices** (`src/audio/voice.js`): Animalese/Simlish gibberish.\n- For each character: base pitch, syllable rate, wave. A syllable is a short layered `SoundDef`, verified to work: `{ wave:'saw', freq:190, freqEnd:170, attack:.005, decay:.09, volume:.3, layers:[{wave:'sine',freq:760,decay:.08,volume:.18},{wave:'sine',freq:1220,decay:.07,volume:.1},{wave:'noise',freq:6000,decay:.012,volume:.08}] }`.\n- Build about 12 of them (vowel formant pairs × consonant noise).\n- `voice.say(character, text)`: for each word, count vowels, hash the word to pick syllables (so a word always \"sounds\" the same), play at the character's pitch with `pitch` offsets for intonation (rise on `?`, fall on `.`, pause at `,`), at the Voices slider's level.\n- Runs over the subtitle's length; soldiers' barks are spatial.\n\nCheck sound by what Web Audio produces (section 24); nobody can listen here.\n\n---\n\n## 23. Packaging\n\nThe deliverable is **one HTML file** at the place your brief names, beside the dev folder. Nothing else is needed at runtime; no CDN is used. It must run from `file://` and from any web server.\n\n**Head order is strict:**\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/* vendor/session.js pasted unmodified, as the first script */</script>\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> ... the tags from section 19 ...\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>GAME_TITLE</title>\n</head><body><div id=\"app\"></div><script type=\"module\">/* the bundle */</script></body></html>\n```\n\n`tools/build.mjs` (the steps I ran, with `</script` escaped; set `OUT` from your brief):\n```js\nconst js = execFileSync('esbuild', ['src/main.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--target=esnext','--legal-comments=inline','--log-level=warning'], { maxBuffer: 1 << 29 }).toString();\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js','utf8');\nconst html = TEMPLATE.replace('%SESSION%', () => session.replace(/<\\/script/gi,'<\\\\/script')).replace('%BUNDLE%', () => js.replace(/<\\/script/gi,'<\\\\/script'));\nwriteFileSync(OUT, html);\n```\nUse function replacers as above, because the engine contains `$` sequences. The bundle is about 5.3 MB before your game is added. Append the licence texts of the engine, three.js and Rapier from the kit's `CREDITS.md` as an HTML comment at the end of the file. esbuild bundles `pixel-engine.js` without you editing it; that satisfies \"keep the engine as it ships\".\n\n`src/main.js` is the only module that starts the engine. Start options:\n```js\nconst OPTIONS = withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera:{ preset:'first', fov:62 }, look: LOOK, touch:false, settings:{ button:false, key:false } });\n```\n`withUrlOptions` keeps the review flags (`?seed=`, `?debug=1`, `?look=`), which are useful and harmless.\n\n---\n\n## 24. Testing\n\nTest as much as you like with headless tools. **One command:** `node tests/run.mjs`. It prints only failures and a one-line count, like `412 passed, 0 failed, 3 suites unchanged`. To rerun only after changing covered code, it hashes each suite's covered files into `tests/.stamps.json` and skips unchanged suites unless given `--all`. `--only <suite>` runs one. **Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.** Log what testing catches in `tests/ISSUES.md`.\n\n**Suites:**\n\n1. **`static` (Node):** `Math.random` only in `vendor/`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`; every string in `src/content/` uses only the font's characters; every model passes `checkModel`, every texture `validateTexture`; every `Song` parses; every sound renders non-silent; every mission's site, tag, line and buddy resolves; every `TUNING` key has a slider or a reason.\n\n2. **`systems` (Node, `Engine.headless`, driven by `e.step` and actions):**\n   - fire head speed 2-5 m/s, can't cross a road without strong wind, burnt can't re-burn, regrowth, save round-trip of the grid\n   - wear math and jam frequency against the formula; jam blocks fire; reload clears it; gun falls apart at 0\n   - hit zones; syrette; surgery; down-with-buddy, down-without\n   - fever: tablet clears it, ignoring collapses and costs stones\n   - perception by stance and night; pass exemption; soldier burn panic\n   - vehicle drive to waypoint, damage thresholds, repair, explode\n   - map up while driving; cursor sets the waypoint\n   - tracker rate; trig note; post cleared then re-manned; rail halt; shop purchase; locker; sleep to hour; save and load\n   - pause (`timeScale` 0), menus by pad, by keyboard, by mouse hover and click\n   - device switch changes `prompts` glyphs; Xbox setting; settings persistence; music 0.1\n   - sliders and Reset; attract starts at frame 601 with no input, not before; any input exits; saves untouched; touch gate state with `matchMedia` stubbed\n   - round rules (solo session): bank, drop, revive, time-up finish\n\n3. **`playthrough` (Node):** a bot plays M0 through both endings, using `dev.teleport` between objectives but real actions for every objective (drive the truck, burn the tanks, plant the charge, choose at the buddy). It runs with god mode on and with buddies alive, and once with one buddy dead. After each mission, assert the world state and Ledger. This is the most important test.\n\n4. **`replay` (Node):** record one mission segment, replay it, compare `engine.fingerprint()`.\n\n5. **`browser` (Playwright, one Chromium):** the built single file on `file://`, 1920×1080, with these flags (the ones `check.mjs` uses; correct frames came from them):\n   `['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan','--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader']`.\n   Check:\n   - no page, console, game or GPU errors; backend WebGPU\n   - `AIHub.active` false\n   - title reached; mouse hover moves focus; click activates\n   - pad stub via `addInitScript` (`navigator.getGamepads = () => [pad]`); keyboard flows\n   - sound by `OfflineAudioContext` rendering of `renderSound` output, or an `AnalyserNode` on `audio.context` after a gesture\n   - a phone profile in Chromium (Playwright's `iPhone 14` device, dropping its default browser type, then tap with `page.touchscreen`): shows the notice and Settings, tap opens Settings, attract runs\n\n6. **`hub` (Playwright + a server you started):** serve a folder holding the built file (renamed or linked as you like) with `vendor/hub/*` on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port (`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port, kill it by its PID at the end), confirm the page's `<title>`, then for each scenario open `harness.html?game=<file>&<scenario>` and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, passing when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true:\n   - `players=2&auto=1`\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n   - `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave`\n   - `players=2&auto=1&scenario=server`\n   Run them one at a time. They take about 15 s each here. Print `__hubCheck.checks` entries that failed.\n\n7. **`link` (Playwright, two pages of one context on `file://`):** two windows on `linkSession` play a round to its end, with a shared fire, a shared case, and same standings.\n\n**Screenshots** only for what a script can't check, at 1920×1080, once each near the end, and whenever you've changed the art they show: title; day first-person with gun; night fire; held map in a truck; Tibor's rack; dialogue; surgery; dev sliders; hub card; ending. Set `e.manual = false`, wait until 10 s or more have passed, and take the screenshot; about 1 frame a second is normal here.\n\n**Stand-ins for what this computer lacks** (your brief): a pad stub, Playwright phone profiles, Web Audio measurement, two pages for a second player, WebKit iPhone only as a rough touch-layout check (the engine needs WebGPU, so expect its failure message and test the notice logic with Chromium's phone profile), and 1920×1080 screenshots for the TV view.\n\n---\n\n## 25. Build order\n\nEach step ends with a playable build and passing tests, and leaves nothing half-done. Do them in order. Write sound, feedback and tests with each feature rather than at the end.\n\n| Step | Do | Done when |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 Pipeline | Pick the title. Unzip the kit. Download `vendor/`. `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs` with the hub suite. A blank `createGame()` with a shared-world round that does nothing | Single file opens on `file://`; **all four hub scenarios pass from day one**. Keep them passing |\n| 1 Walk | Terrain, roads, river, sky, wind, grass, trees. Player, `installLook`, actions, pad codes, device tracking, HUD basics, pause | You can walk the Kettle by pad or mouse |\n| 2 Shoot | Hands rig and `tractor` first, soldier rig, one AI squad, damage, health, syrette, wear, jam, reload. SFX for each | A fight at P2 is fun on its own. Add the other guns |\n| 3 Burn | Fire grid, flare, bottle, flamethrower, scars, regrowth, soldier panic, day and night. Fire sounds | Lighting a field at night makes you stop and watch |\n| 4 Drive | Vehicles, driving view, damage, repair, patrols, posts, train | Run a checkpoint in a pickup and break down after |\n| 5 World | All sites, safehouses, Tibor, stones, tracker, fever, tablets, surgery, monocular, held map, rail halts, trig points, save and load | A whole session without missions works |\n| 6 Story | Mission runner, dialogue, voices, buddies, rescues, M0 to End, Ledger, endings. Playthrough suite | A bot and you both finish it |\n| 7 Shell | Title, all menus, settings, glyph sets, About, touch gate, attract, dev mode | Every screen works by pad, keyboard and mouse |\n| 8 Multiplayer | Sim/view puppets, host, client, sessions, Cash Run, card, solo bots, link | Hub and link suites pass with a real round |\n| 9 Polish | All music, ambience, feel, art passes, balance with the sliders, screenshots | You'd show it to someone |\n| 10 Ship | Final build, About pages, exit interview, chat reply, `node tests/run.mjs --all` | Section 27 is all true |\n\n---\n\n## 26. Cut order\n\nIf you can't finish everything, cut whole things from the top of this list. Don't leave stubs, and remove all traces (menu entries, map marks, lines). Keep every feature in the demo reachable and shown.\n\n1. Fauna (vultures first).\n2. Same-computer link (keep hub and solo).\n3. The game's own hub card (use the hub's card: `card:false`).\n4. Chase camera; dust weather.\n5. Mercy Runs after the first; trig points down to 3; cases down to 6.\n6. M5 (keep its Mags plan in M6 instead).\n7. M4 (give the tracker in M3; use the MG in M7; Dov joins at the bar).\n8. M8 (the column just starts walking after M7).\n9. Shared-world → ghost → parallel (section 19).\n\nNever cut: fire, wear and jams, fever, the held map, buddy rescue and the finish-it choice, Harrow's twist, dev mode with sliders, attract mode, glyphs and native mouse, touch gate, the hub check, music at 10%, the voices.\n\n---\n\n## 27. Done, reports, coverage\n\n**Done means:** every line below is true and you've checked it.\n- [ ] Your title, chosen by the brief's procedure, appears on the title screen and in `<title>`.\n- [ ] The one HTML file runs from `file://` with nothing else beside it.\n- [ ] `node tests/run.mjs --all` reports 0 failed.\n- [ ] All four hub scenarios pass, and the game still runs outside a session.\n- [ ] You finished the game yourself by bot, both endings.\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features anywhere.\n- [ ] Every screen shows native prompts for pad (PlayStation and Xbox glyphs) and keyboard/mouse.\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots.\n- [ ] `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit's.\n\n**About and Credits screen** (in-game, paged, reached from main menu and pause; text in `src/content/about.js`). Your brief marks this as critical. It must show:\n- **Source Information,** with every field your brief lists: Date (from your brief), Source (Far Cry 2), Plan (the file name of this plan as you received it), Genre and Perspective, Tech (engine and its version, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, esbuild, your additions), Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model (from your brief), and Usage: *added on import*. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback:** your brief's questions and your honest answers, in order, each on its own page. Don't estimate cost.\n- **Credits:** Pixel Engine (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the AI Hub session client, and \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\".\n\n**Your final chat reply** repeats the same Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback.\n\n**The exit interview file** your brief specifies goes beside the HTML, in the exact JSON shape it gives, every value filled, and must parse. Suggested catalogue values: type `Game`; genre `Shooter`; source `Far Cry 2`; series `Far Cry`; players `1–4`; tags such as `first-person`, `pixel art`, `co-op`, `versus`, `vehicles`, `story`; the name is your title.\n\n**Where each line of your brief is covered:**\n\n| Brief item | Section |\n| --- | --- |\n| Source, original game, capture world, characters, themes, tone | 1, 2, 15 |\n| Genre, perspective | 1 |\n| Title by random pick (left to you) | top, 25 |\n| Research | 3 (done for you; research more if you want) |\n| Single HTML package, CDN allowance, bundle into root | 23 |\n| Engine kit kept as shipped, systems added on top | 3, 4, 23 |\n| Art direction: native base, new assets, library for generic props | 20 |\n| Animation: native base, new animations | 21 |\n| Audio in code, Animalese voices, music 10% | 22 |\n| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 19 |\n| Dev subfolder, what not to read, shared ports and processes | 4, 24 |\n| Performance out of scope | 3 |\n| Testing: headless, scripted playthrough, one command, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | 24 |\n| Effort | Yours to spend; 25 and 26 order it |\n| This computer and its stand-ins | 3, 4, 24 |\n| Feature-complete vertical slice, infrastructure for later build-out | 4, 14, 25 |\n| Dev mode from pause, standard tools, live sliders, reset | 17 |\n| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits | 18 |\n| Narrative tone | 15 |\n| 10-foot controller first, native keyboard and mouse, hover focus, glyphs, Xbox option | 16 |\n| No dense web-style screens | 16 |\n| Touch-only notice, settings button, attract runs | 16, 18 |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | top, 26, 27 |\n| Source Information, feedback sections, chat reply, About screen | 27 |\n| Exit interview | 27 |\n","numLines":330,"startLine":798,"totalLines":1127}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J6xYzFAqSufCudAtn4rJME","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
